  "raw_text": "Step 1: Let \\( x = (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\). We need to find the greatest integer less than \\( x \\).\n\nStep 2: Consider the conjugate of \\( \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5} \\), which is \\( \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5} \\). Note that \\( 0 < \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5} < 1 \\).\n\nStep 3: Calculate \\( (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 \\). Since \\( 0 < \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5} < 1 \\), it follows that \\( 0 < (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 < 1 \\).\n\nStep 4: Add the two expressions \\( (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\) and \\( (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 \\):\n\\[ (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 + (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 \\]\nThis sum will be an integer because all irrational parts will cancel out.\n\nStep 5: Expand \\( (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\) and \\( (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 \\) using the binomial theorem. The terms involving odd powers of \\( \\sqrt{7} \\) and \\( \\sqrt{5} \\) will cancel out when added together, leaving only terms with even powers.\n\nStep 6: The resulting expression will be:\n\\[ 2 \\left( \\binom{6}{0} 7^3 5^0 + \\binom{6}{2} 7^2 5^1 + \\binom{6}{4} 7^1 5^2 + \\binom{6}{6} 7^0 5^3 \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( 343 + 945 + 315 + 125 \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\times 1738 = 3476 \\]\n\nStep 7: Since \\( 0 < (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 < 1 \\), we have:\n\\[ 3475 < (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 < 3476 \\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{3475}<|im_end|>",
